    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogies. Let me break it down for you in plain terms.\n\nWhen you set up an escrow account, the funds are typically held by a third-party escrow agent, such as a bank or a title company. The escrow agent acts as an intermediary between you and the seller, holding the funds until the closing date.\n\nHere's a step-by-step explanation of the process:\n\n1. Your bank transfers the funds to the escrow agent's account, which is usually a separate account from your own.\n2. The escrow agent holds the funds in a trust account, which is separate from your bank account and the seller's bank account.\n3. On the closing date, the escrow agent disburses the funds to the seller, minus any applicable fees and taxes.\n4. The seller then transfers the ownership of the property to you, and the escrow agent releases its hold on the funds.\n\nThink of it like a 'goblin's ledger' – the escrow agent keeps track of all the transactions, ensuring that the funds are transferred correctly and that all parties are paid in full.\n\nIn Toronto, the escrow process typically involves a combination of electronic funds transfer (EFT",
